[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"ISP: Adapter Pattern and Decoupling Systems"},{"order":2,"type":"subheading","text":"Introduction to the Interface Segregation Principle (ISP)"},{"order":3,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Interface Segregation Principle states that no client should be forced to depend on methods it does not use. In large-scale enterprise architectures, violating ISP leads to bloated interfaces, tight coupling, and rigid systems that are difficult to refactor and test."},{"order":4,"type":"subheading","text":"The Problem: Fat Interfaces"},{"order":5,"type":"paragraph","text":"Consider a scenario where we define a monolithic interface for multiple types of cloud storage operations. Clients that only need to read data are forced to implement write and delete methods."},{"order":6,"type":"code","text":"public interface CloudStorage {
    void uploadFile(String path, byte[] data);
    byte[] downloadFile(String path);
    void deleteFile(String path);
    void generateSharedLink(String path);
}"},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Applying ISP by Splitting Interfaces"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"To adhere to ISP, we break down the monolithic interface into smaller, role-specific interfaces. This ensures that classes only implement the contracts relevant to their operational domain."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"public interface FileUploader {
    void uploadFile(String path, byte[] data);
}

public interface FileDownloader {
    byte[] downloadFile(String path);
}

public interface FileDeleter {
    void deleteFile(String path);
}"},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"Decoupling Systems Using the Adapter Pattern"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"When integrating third-party libraries or legacy systems that expose fat interfaces, we can use the Adapter Pattern. The Adapter translates the wide interface of the legacy system into the narrow, segregated interfaces expected by our domain logic."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"public class LegacyCloudService {
    public void legacyUpload(String p, byte[] d) { /* Implementation */ }
    public byte[] legacyDownload(String p) { return new byte[0]; }
    public void legacyDelete(String p) { /* Implementation */ }
}

public class CloudStorageAdapter implements FileUploader, FileDownloader {
    private final LegacyCloudService legacyService;

    public CloudStorageAdapter(LegacyCloudService legacyService) {
        this.legacyService = legacyService;
    }

    @Override
    public void uploadFile(String path, byte[] data) {
        legacyService.legacyUpload(path, data);
    }

    @Override
    public byte[] downloadFile(String path) {
        return legacyService.legacyDownload(path);
    }
}"},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Benefits of Combining ISP and the Adapter Pattern"},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"High Cohesion: Components have clearly defined, single-purpose responsibilities."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Loose Coupling: Domain logic depends on abstractions, not concrete third-party implementations."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Enhanced Testability: Smaller interfaces are significantly easier to mock during unit testing."}]
